In this lesson, we'll look at populations versus samples, two words you'll use all the time in statistics. The population is the entire group you actually want to learn about, like every Year 8 student in a school, or every Australian. The sample, on the other hand, is a smaller group you actually collect data from, since measuring everyone is usually impossible. So if you want the average height of Year 8s, the population is all of them, and the sample might be the forty kids you happen to measure. Why does this matter? Because any number you calculate is either a parameter, which uses the whole population, or a statistic, which comes from the sample. And conclusions about a population are only as trustworthy as the sample representing it. In practice, populations are often too big, too far, or too costly, so we rely on samples, knowing that shortcut introduces some uncertainty. Now let's see it in action.